<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Evaluate the Integral by any strategy of Integration</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Use a trig substitution to evaluate</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@}{@func@}dy=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] (round off at 4 decimalplaces)&nbsp;</p><p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<p>In this case we’ll need the following trigonometric substitution</p>
<p>\(y={@sqrt(bb)@} \sec \theta\quad\implies dy={@sqrt(bb)@} \sec \theta \tan \theta \,d\theta \)</p>
<p>Now we need to use substitution to eliminate the root</p>
<p>\(\sqrt{y^2-{@bb@}}=\sqrt{{@bb@}\sec^2 \theta-{@bb@}}={@sqrt(bb)@}\sqrt{\tan^2 \theta}={@sqrt(bb)@} |\tan \theta|\)</p>
<p>We get the limits on \(\theta\)</p>
<p>\(y={@lower_limit@}:\quad {@lower_limit@}={@sqrt(bb)@}\sec\theta \quad \implies \sec\theta={@lower_limit/sqrt(bb)@}\quad\implies\theta=\sec^{-1}({@lower_limit/sqrt(bb)@})={@ta_1@}\)</p>
<p>\(y={@upper_limit@}:\quad{@upper_limit@}={@sqrt(bb)@}\sec\theta \quad \implies \sec\theta={@upper_limit/sqrt(bb)@}\quad\implies\theta=sec^{-1}({@upper_limit/sqrt(bb)@})={@ta_2@}\)</p>
<p>So, \({\theta}\)'s for this problem are in the range \({@ta_1@}\leq {\theta} \leq {@ta_2@}\) and these are in the first quadrant. In the first quadrant we know that tangent is positive. This gives,</p>
<p>\(\sqrt{y^2-{@bb@}}={@sqrt(bb)@}\tan{\theta}\)</p>
<p>Now, doing the actual substitution,</p>
<p>
\(
\begin{align}
  \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@}{@func@}\,dy &amp;=\int_{@ta_1@}^{@ta_2@}{\frac{{@aa@}}{{@sqrt(bb)@}^4\sec^4{\theta}({@sqrt(bb)@}\tan{\theta})}({@sqrt(bb)@}\sec{\theta}\tan{\theta})}\,d{\theta} \\
  &amp;= {@aa/(sqrt(bb)^4)@}\int_{@ta_1@}^{@ta_2@}{\frac{1}{\sec^3{\theta}}}\,d{\theta}
\end{align}
\)
</p>
<p>We now evaluate the integral in terms of cosine:
</p>
<p>
 \(
\begin{align}
  \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@}{@func@}\,dy  &amp;= {@aa/(sqrt(bb)^4)@}\int_{@ta_1@}^{@ta_2@}{\cos^3{\theta}}\,d{\theta}\\
  &amp;= {@aa/(sqrt(bb)^4)@}\int_{@ta_1@}^{@ta_2@}{\cos{\theta}\cos^2{\theta}}\,d{\theta}\\
 &amp;= {@aa/(sqrt(bb)^4)@}\int_{@ta_1@}^{@ta_2@}{(1-\sin^2{\theta})\cos{\theta}}\,d{\theta}&amp;&amp; \text{Since} \cos^2{\theta}=1-\sin^2{\theta} 
\end{align}
\)
</p>

Taking \(u=\sin{\theta}\quad du=\cos{\theta}\,d{\theta}\)
<p>\(
\begin{align}
  \int_{@lower_limit@}^{@upper_limit@}{@func@}\,dy &amp;={@aa/ (sqrt(bb))^4@} \int_{\sin{@ta_1@}}^{\sin{@ta_2@}}(1-u^2)\,du\\
&amp;={@aa/ (sqrt(bb))^4@}\left [ u-\frac{1}{3}u^3 \right] _{{@ta_3@}}^{\{@ta_4@}}\\
&amp;={@ta@}
\end{align}
 \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
